nue recovering to $6.58B in Q4 2027 with EPS of $0.17 — implies a material earnings improvement, but the 2026 growth projections already show a worrying Q1 2026 revenue decline of 26.6% YoY and EPS growth of -83.3%, which suggests the ramp-up is back-end loaded and carries execution risk. The DCF intrinsic value of $6.91 implies the market is pricing in a recovery scenario that has not yet materialized in reported financials. The 52-analyst consensus is a 'Buy' but with a median price target of $14.00, actually below the current trading price of $15.63, indicating even bulls are not aligned with the current valuation. [Source: FMP /analyst-estimates, /price-target, DCF model]\n\nThe macroeconomic backdrop of 10Y Treasury at 4.5% and still-elevated rate environment constrains Nokia's valuation multiple and increases the cost of customer capex deferral, a key demand risk for the company's core Mobile Networks segment. Overall, Nokia's financial health is adequate but not robust — the balance sheet is a genuine asset, but margin instability and valuation concerns cap the score.",
