The core valuation debate is entirely predicated on Neutron: the medium-lift rocket currently in development targeting its first launch in 2025-2026. Bulls argue that a successful Neutron launch would unlock a $10B+ addressable market in government and commercial medium-lift, potentially generating $1-2B in annual launch revenue at 50%+ margins within 5-7 years. Under this scenario — call it the 'Neutron succeeds on schedule' base case — one could construct a revenue path reaching $4-5B by 2032 with 40% operating margins, generating ~$1.6-2.0B EBIT. Discounting at 12% WACC with a 25x terminal EBIT multiple yields an enterprise value of approximately $15-20B, still 50-60% below current market cap. To reach $40B in intrinsic value, one needs Neutron to not just succeed but achieve SpaceX-like launch cadence and pricing power within a decade — an outcome that has exactly one historical precedent and which the second-mover faces structural disadvantages replicating.

