quarter in recent quarters) and widening FCF losses. First launch has not occurred. Historical precedent for new launch vehicle development suggests 12-24 month slippage is the norm, not the exception. A 3-year delay (to 2029-2030 first commercial operations) would mean RKLB burns an additional $800M-$1.2B in FCF beyond current projections — potentially exhausting the current cash cushion and requiring dilutive equity raises [Source: FMP /cash-flow-statement]. Each dilutive raise would further compress per-share intrinsic value. The probability-weighted loss from this scenario, assuming even 40% probability of a 3+ year delay, represents enormous expected value destruction at current prices.\n\n**Macro Context:** Declining Fed funds rate (3.64% from 4.09% six months prior) provides modest multiple support for high-growth pre-profitability names [Source: FRED fed_funds_rate]. M2 expansion and easing monetary conditions are incremental positives. However, with 10-year Treasury at 4.31%, the discount rate for a terminal-value-heavy story like RKLB remains punishing, and GDP growth deceleration in the most recent quarter (0.5%) introduces defense budget risk for government contracts [Source: FRED treasury_10y, gdp_growth].\n\n**Verdict:** RKLB has genuine technological differentiation, improving unit economics, and sufficient liquidity to execute. The base franchise alone, however, cannot sustain the current valuation — durability as an investment (not as a company) scores materially below what the gross margin trajectory and backlog momentum might suggest in isolation. The thesis is Neutron or bust.",
